WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:05.104 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 Beneath the clear blue waters</i>

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:10.309 line:0%
<i> of the Mediterranean lie
 treasures of ancient empires,</i>

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:15.515 line:0%
<i> relics of their bloody wars,</i>

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:19.819 line:0%
<i> and secrets of the seismic
 forces that shaped them,</i>

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:24.390 line:0%
<i> lost beneath the waves...</i>

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.293 line:0%
<i> until now.</i>

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:30.062 line:0%
<i> Imagine if we could
 empty the oceans,</i>

00:00:30.062 --> 00:00:32.264 line:0%
<i> letting the water drain away</i>

00:00:32.264 --> 00:00:36.669 line:0%
<i> to reveal the secrets
 of the seafloor.</i>

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:39.239 line:0%
<i> Now, we can.</i>

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:45.611 line:0%
<i> Using the latest underwater
 scanning technology,</i>

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:48.180 line:0%
<i> piercing the deep oceans,</i>

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.385 line:0%
<i> and turning accurate data
 into 3D images.</i>

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.257 line:0%
<i> This time,
 what apocalyptic disaster</i>

00:00:58.257 --> 00:01:03.362 line:0%
<i> triggered the collapse
 of Europe's first civilization?</i>

00:01:03.362 --> 00:01:07.500 line:0%
<i> Can an extraordinary
 2,500-year-old shipwreck</i>

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.370 line:0%
<i> unlock the secrets
 of Ancient Greece?</i>

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.806 line:0%
<i> How did these deadly objects</i>

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:17.377 line:0%
<i> turn Ancient Rome
 into a superpower?</i>

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:20.980 line:0%
<i> And why does the Sin City
 of the Roman Empire</i>

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.050 line:0%
<i> lie abandoned
 beneath the waves?</i>

00:01:26.685 --> 00:01:29.121 line:0%
(music)

00:01:29.121 --> 00:01:34.760 line:0%
(music)

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:41.701 line:0%
(music)

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:45.004 line:0%
<i> Ancient people call it
 the Middle Sea,</i>

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:47.340 line:0%
<i> the center of the known world.</i>

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.742 line:0%
<i> The Egyptians,
 Greeks and Romans</i>

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:54.514 line:0%
<i> build mighty civilizations
 upon its shores.</i>

00:01:54.514 --> 00:01:59.619 line:0%
<i> Empires battle for supremacy
 across its waters.</i>

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:03.989 line:0%
<i> Cities grow rich and powerful
 through trade.</i>

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:07.660 line:0%
<i> The Mediterranean becomes
 a superhighway,</i>

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:12.131 line:0%
<i> connecting cultures that
 will shape the modern world.</i>

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:15.801 line:0%
<i> But only by draining the sea
 can we reveal</i>

00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:19.839 line:0%
<i> its biggest and most
 terrifying secret.</i>

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:23.509 line:0%
<i> What happened on this
 spectacular Greek island</i>

00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:26.679 line:0%
<i> to doom an entire civilization?</i>

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.917 line:0%
COSTAS SYNOLAKIS: They must have
thought this was it,

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:35.021 line:0%
the end of the world.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:38.624 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: 3,600 years ago.</i>

00:02:38.624 --> 00:02:42.662 line:0%
<i> 15 centuries before
 the Roman Empire.</i>

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:46.966 line:0%
<i> A mysterious people dominate
 the Mediterranean.</i>

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:50.870 line:0%
<i> We call them the Minoans.</i>

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.873 line:0%
<i> Their home is on Crete.</i>

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:57.843 line:0%
<i> Here they build magnificent
 temples and palaces,</i>

00:02:57.843 --> 00:03:02.348 line:0%
<i> and decorate them
 with stunning frescoes.</i>

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:06.719 line:0%
<i> Celebrating their love
 of life and of nature.</i>

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:13.993 line:0%
<i> But there's a darker side, too.</i>

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:16.528 line:0%
<i> The Minoan royal palace
 at Knossos</i>

00:03:16.528 --> 00:03:19.698 line:0%
<i> is said to contain a labyrinth.</i>

00:03:19.698 --> 00:03:22.334 line:0%
<i> Home to the Minotaur.</i>

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:24.136 line:0%
<i> A fearsome creature...</i>

00:03:24.136 --> 00:03:26.539 line:0%
<i> part man, part bull,</i>

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:30.677 line:0%
<i> with a terrible appetite
 for human flesh.</i>

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:35.047 line:0%
<i> But apart from a grisly myth,</i>

00:03:35.047 --> 00:03:39.985 line:0%
<i> much about the Minoan world
 still remains a mystery.</i>

00:03:39.985 --> 00:03:41.787 line:0%
MICHAEL SCOTT: We can't
decipher their language.

00:03:41.787 --> 00:03:43.556 line:0%
We only have their
archaeological remains,

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:45.024 line:0%
but what that tells us, I think,

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:47.994 line:0%
is that they were
extremely imaginative,

00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:50.129 line:0%
they were extremely adventurous,

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.397 line:0%
and that they developed

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:54.067 line:0%
a sophisticated
hierarchical society

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:58.037 line:0%
that was capable of producing
elements of art and architecture

00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:01.007 line:0%
which still astound us today.

00:04:03.008 --> 00:04:05.811 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Historians do know
 that the Minoans spread</i>

00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:08.081 line:0%
<i> across the Mediterranean,</i>

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:12.918 line:0%
<i> trading olive oil and pottery
 for gold and ivory,</i>

00:04:12.918 --> 00:04:16.889 line:0%
<i> growing ever richer
 and more powerful.</i>

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:20.693 line:0%
<i> And then,
 in the 15th century BC,</i>

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.863 line:0%
<i> their ancient civilization
 begins to fade</i>

00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:26.599 line:0%
<i> from the pages of history.</i>

00:04:28.835 --> 00:04:32.272 line:0%
<i> For centuries,
 no one knows why.</i>

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.443 line:0%
<i> Until scientists start
 to look closely</i>

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:41.047 line:0%
<i> at the nearby island
 of Santorini.</i>

00:04:43.782 --> 00:04:48.954 line:0%
SYNOLAKIS: Santorini, it has
this very, very calm water.

00:04:48.954 --> 00:04:52.325 line:0%
It's almost like it plays
with you and deceives you.

00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:55.661 line:0%
Looking at this view,
you would never imagine

00:04:55.661 --> 00:05:00.766 line:0%
how dangerous it once was
and how dangerous it is.

00:05:00.766 --> 00:05:02.301 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The island is famous</i>

00:05:02.301 --> 00:05:05.071 line:0%
<i> for its spectacular,
 jagged cliffs,</i>

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:10.475 line:0%
<i> which tower above
 a beautiful natural harbor.</i>

00:05:10.475 --> 00:05:12.644 line:0%
<i> But what created them?</i>

00:05:12.644 --> 00:05:17.250 line:0%
<i> And can they help explain
 the downfall of the Minoans?</i>

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:26.291 line:0%
<i> Marine geologist Evi Nomikou
 believes that crucial clues</i>

00:05:26.291 --> 00:05:30.863 line:0%
<i> may lie deep beneath
 Santorini's tranquil waters.</i>

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:32.364 line:0%
EVI NOMIKOU:
As I was born in Santorini,

00:05:32.364 --> 00:05:38.404 line:0%
I wanted to study the area
to reveal their secret.

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:42.708 line:0%
Being a marine geologist means
that you're having access

00:05:42.708 --> 00:05:45.143 line:0%
to the mystic world
of the seafloor,

00:05:45.143 --> 00:05:48.981 line:0%
so you can see features
that nobody else can see.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:51.984 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 She harnesses the latest</i>

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:54.587 line:0%
<i> multi-beam sonar technology</i>

00:05:54.587 --> 00:05:58.524 line:0%
<i> to scan the depths
 of the huge bay.</i>

00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:03.428 line:0%
<i> By transforming her data
 into powerful computer imagery,</i>

00:06:03.428 --> 00:06:07.966 line:0%
<i> it's possible to drain away the
 waters of the Mediterranean...</i>

00:06:07.966 --> 00:06:12.971 line:0%
(music)

00:06:12.971 --> 00:06:17.776 line:0%
<i> ...and reveal Santorini's
 terrifying secret.</i>

00:06:17.776 --> 00:06:22.782 line:0%
<i> The sheer cliffs tower
 1,000 feet above sea level</i>

00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:27.420 line:0%
<i> and drop a further 1,000 feet
 to the seafloor.</i>

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.254 line:0%
<i> Framing a vast basin</i>

00:06:29.254 --> 00:06:35.460 line:0%
<i> large enough to hold
 10,000 Olympic stadiums.</i>

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:39.398 line:0%
<i> The basin is a huge crater,</i>

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:45.070 line:0%
<i> and Santorini itself is the
 remnant of a gigantic volcano.</i>

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:48.574 line:0%
(music)

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:50.743 line:0%
<i> But that's not all.</i>

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:52.811 line:0%
<i> On the rim of the crater,</i>

00:06:52.811 --> 00:06:56.915 line:0%
<i> more evidence of Santorini's
 violent past.</i>

00:06:56.915 --> 00:07:01.486 line:0%
(music)

00:07:01.486 --> 00:07:04.490 line:0%
(screams)

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:08.160 line:0%
<i> Undiscovered until 1967,</i>

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:10.863 line:0%
<i> these shattered ruins
 are all that remain</i>

00:07:10.863 --> 00:07:13.366 line:0%
<i> of a once-thriving city,</i>

00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:16.902 line:0%
<i> known today as Akrotiri.</i>

00:07:16.902 --> 00:07:19.772 line:0%
<i> It was destroyed when
 the volcano erupted,</i>

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:23.809 line:0%
<i> and buried under so much ash
 that it remained hidden</i>

00:07:23.809 --> 00:07:26.945 line:0%
<i> for 3,500 years.</i>

00:07:26.945 --> 00:07:29.214 line:0%
LEFTERIS ZORZOS: It's almost
like a window back in time,

00:07:29.214 --> 00:07:33.185 line:0%
where you can see how it was
when it was destroyed.

00:07:33.185 --> 00:07:35.020 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: And deep in the ruins</i>

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:39.158 line:0%
<i> archaeologists discover
 something remarkable.</i>

00:07:40.759 --> 00:07:44.163 line:0%
(rumbling)

00:07:44.163 --> 00:07:46.665 line:0%
<i> These stone steps
 were not broken</i>

00:07:46.665 --> 00:07:49.301 line:0%
<i> by the ash and lava
 from a volcano.</i>

00:07:49.301 --> 00:07:54.573 line:0%
(rumbling)

00:07:54.573 --> 00:07:59.411 line:0%
<i> They were shattered
 by an earthquake.</i>

00:07:59.411 --> 00:08:01.246 line:0%
ZORZOS: When the earthquake
happened,

00:08:01.246 --> 00:08:04.450 line:0%
everyone fled their town,

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.952 line:0%
but then they came back to start
rebuilding their homes,

00:08:06.952 --> 00:08:08.921 line:0%
and this is exactly
what we're seeing here.

00:08:11.957 --> 00:08:14.060 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Believing
 the danger over,</i>

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:16.795 line:0%
<i> people move furniture
 into the streets,</i>

00:08:16.795 --> 00:08:20.266 line:0%
<i> so they can start repairing
 their houses.</i>

00:08:20.266 --> 00:08:23.569 line:0%
ZORZOS: We're seeing the beds
placed outside of their homes,

00:08:23.569 --> 00:08:26.004 line:0%
we're seeing the stones and mud

00:08:26.004 --> 00:08:30.309 line:0%
getting ready to be used
to rebuild these homes.

00:08:30.309 --> 00:08:34.112 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But then they are
 struck by an apocalypse.</i>

00:08:34.112 --> 00:08:37.450 line:0%
(rumbling)

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:39.418 line:0%
(explosion)

00:08:42.821 --> 00:08:46.359 line:0%
<i> The first stage of the eruption
 is so powerful</i>

00:08:46.359 --> 00:08:48.527 line:0%
<i> that it engulfs Akrotiri</i>

00:08:48.527 --> 00:08:53.065 line:0%
<i> and suffocates the whole island
 in a thick layer of debris.</i>

00:08:56.401 --> 00:08:59.772 line:0%
<i> And the date of this
 cataclysmic eruption?</i>

00:08:59.772 --> 00:09:05.277 line:0%
<i> Around 1625 BC, the same time
 as the Minoans</i>

00:09:05.277 --> 00:09:09.414 line:0%
<i> begin to disappear from
 the pages of history.</i>

00:09:09.414 --> 00:09:12.451 line:0%
<i> But how could a single eruption
 trigger the collapse</i>

00:09:12.451 --> 00:09:19.391 line:0%
<i> of a great civilization based
 on an island 70 miles away?</i>

00:09:19.391 --> 00:09:22.461 line:0%
<i> Until recently most scientists
 have focused</i>

00:09:22.461 --> 00:09:26.398 line:0%
<i> only on the evidence
 above ground.</i>

00:09:26.398 --> 00:09:29.935 line:0%
<i> But Evi Nomikou believes
 that once again</i>

00:09:29.935 --> 00:09:34.006 line:0%
<i> the real clues lie underwater.</i>

00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:35.474 line:0%
EVI NOMIKOU: Scientists
have been occupied

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:38.276 line:0%
studying only
the on-land geology,

00:09:38.276 --> 00:09:40.980 line:0%
so we are starting
mapping the seafloor,

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.181 line:0%
in order to find out
the total volume

00:09:43.181 --> 00:09:46.285 line:0%
of that big, destructive
eruption.

00:09:48.286 --> 00:09:50.856 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Evi heads outside
 the great crater</i>

00:09:50.856 --> 00:09:53.859 line:0%
<i> to hunt for new evidence
 on the seabed.</i>

00:09:56.428 --> 00:10:00.732 line:0%
<i> And what she finds
 is extraordinary.</i>

00:10:00.732 --> 00:10:04.070 line:0%
<i> Proof of the sheer scale
 of this eruption.</i>

00:10:06.105 --> 00:10:08.607 line:0%
<i> As the waters of
 the Mediterranean recede</i>

00:10:08.607 --> 00:10:13.678 line:0%
<i> still further, they reveal
 wide stone terraces,</i>

00:10:13.678 --> 00:10:17.249 line:0%
<i> fanning out from
 the mouth of the volcano...</i>

00:10:17.249 --> 00:10:20.920 line:0%
<i> the size of 20-story buildings.</i>

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:27.393 line:0%
<i> They point to one cause.</i>

00:10:32.264 --> 00:10:35.333 line:0%
<i> They're called
 pyroclastic flows:</i>

00:10:35.333 --> 00:10:40.038 line:0%
<i> torrents of superheated gas
 and molten rock.</i>

00:10:40.038 --> 00:10:43.509 line:0%
NOMIKOU: The pyroclastic flow
can cover everything.

00:10:43.509 --> 00:10:45.477 line:0%
They travel like a hurricane.

00:10:45.477 --> 00:10:48.948 line:0%
They can destroy
everything on their path

00:10:48.948 --> 00:10:52.985 line:0%
because of the high temperature,
up to 1,000 Celsius.

00:10:57.156 --> 00:10:58.957 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 When they hit the sea,</i>

00:10:58.957 --> 00:11:05.798 line:0%
<i> the pyroclastic flows cool and
 become solid ramparts of rock.</i>

00:11:05.798 --> 00:11:10.035 line:0%
<i> Around Santorini, they stretch
 for a staggering 20 miles</i>

00:11:10.035 --> 00:11:13.338 line:0%
<i> in every direction.</i>

00:11:13.338 --> 00:11:17.977 line:0%
<i> Evidence of multiple eruptions
 lasting for days.</i>

00:11:20.645 --> 00:11:22.914 line:0%
<i> By measuring
 the stone terraces,</i>

00:11:22.914 --> 00:11:26.118 line:0%
<i> scientists calculate that
 the volcano throws out</i>

00:11:26.118 --> 00:11:29.354 line:0%
<i> 14 cubic miles of debris.</i>

00:11:29.354 --> 00:11:34.293 line:0%
<i> An eruption far more powerful
 than they had ever imagined.</i>

00:11:34.293 --> 00:11:37.563 line:0%
<i> It's one of the biggest
 volcanic explosions</i>

00:11:37.563 --> 00:11:39.999 line:0%
<i> in the history of the planet.</i>

00:11:39.999 --> 00:11:42.635 line:0%
(explosion)

00:11:47.639 --> 00:11:51.643 line:0%
SYNOLAKIS: Let's try to imagine
what this eruption looked like.

00:11:51.643 --> 00:11:55.214 line:0%
If you were sitting somewhere in
any of the neighboring islands,

00:11:55.214 --> 00:11:59.184 line:0%
it would have appeared
like the end of the world.

00:11:59.184 --> 00:12:01.487 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: At first
 the volcano blasts out</i>

00:12:01.487 --> 00:12:07.592 line:0%
<i> a column of superheated debris
 more than 20 miles high.</i>

00:12:07.592 --> 00:12:11.029 line:0%
SYNOLAKIS: This huge funnel
of black ash and cloud

00:12:11.029 --> 00:12:14.700 line:0%
could have been seen going
all the way to the sky.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.803 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Some of the volcanic
 plume falls to Earth</i>

00:12:17.803 --> 00:12:21.707 line:0%
<i> many miles from Santorini.</i>

00:12:21.707 --> 00:12:23.676 line:0%
SYNOLAKIS: It would have been
raining pumice

00:12:23.676 --> 00:12:25.477 line:0%
on the surrounding islands.

00:12:25.477 --> 00:12:27.846 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But now
 the volcano unleashes</i>

00:12:27.846 --> 00:12:30.749 line:0%
<i> its most devastating surprise,</i>

00:12:30.749 --> 00:12:36.155 line:0%
<i> and in its path lies Crete,
 the center of the Minoan world.</i>

00:12:40.325 --> 00:12:42.261 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: 70 miles
 from Santorini,</i>

00:12:42.261 --> 00:12:46.765 line:0%
<i> the Minoans on Crete see the
 soaring column of ash and smoke</i>

00:12:46.765 --> 00:12:49.601 line:0%
<i> from the erupting volcano.</i>

00:12:49.601 --> 00:12:54.539 line:0%
<i> But they have no idea
 of the disaster to come.</i>

00:12:54.539 --> 00:12:59.311 line:0%
<i> The volcano blasts millions
 of tons of lava into the sea.</i>

00:12:59.311 --> 00:13:03.916 line:0%
<i> Triggering wave after wave
 of powerful tsunamis.</i>

00:13:05.583 --> 00:13:07.853 line:0%
SYNOLAKIS: When the tsunami
arrived in Crete,

00:13:07.853 --> 00:13:10.288 line:0%
they were probably taken
totally by surprise.

00:13:10.288 --> 00:13:14.126 line:0%
Imagine seeing
this wall of water,

00:13:14.126 --> 00:13:18.196 line:0%
in some places ten meters high,
advancing in.

00:13:18.196 --> 00:13:21.032 line:0%
It must have looked like
this was the end of the world.

00:13:21.032 --> 00:13:22.467 line:0%
Totally unexpected.

00:13:22.467 --> 00:13:25.437 line:0%
The wrath of the gods.

00:13:25.437 --> 00:13:28.773 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Entire coastal
 communities are swept away</i>

00:13:28.773 --> 00:13:33.145 line:0%
<i> by waves up to 30 feet tall.</i>

00:13:33.145 --> 00:13:38.050 line:0%
<i> Ports are destroyed
 and ships smashed to pieces.</i>

00:13:44.823 --> 00:13:48.593 line:0%
<i> And the gods
 aren't finished yet.</i>

00:13:48.593 --> 00:13:51.229 line:0%
<i> After the eruption
 and the tsunamis,</i>

00:13:51.229 --> 00:13:54.032 line:0%
<i> another disaster is looming.</i>

00:13:54.032 --> 00:13:57.569 line:0%
<i> Clouds of volcanic ash
 cast a deadly pall</i>

00:13:57.569 --> 00:14:02.708 line:0%
<i> over the whole Mediterranean,
 dramatically cooling the Earth.</i>

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:08.713 line:0%
SCOTT: There were a series
of effects from the eruption

00:14:08.713 --> 00:14:13.986 line:0%
that together fatally weakened
the Minoan civilization.

00:14:13.986 --> 00:14:16.521 line:0%
A tsunami event, a divine event.

00:14:16.521 --> 00:14:19.424 line:0%
The destabilization
of their economy,

00:14:19.424 --> 00:14:22.594 line:0%
the failure of harvests
over several years.

00:14:22.594 --> 00:14:26.932 line:0%
That was the moment when Minoan
civilization started to die.

00:14:29.001 --> 00:14:31.236 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Without their ports
 and ships,</i>

00:14:31.236 --> 00:14:35.207 line:0%
<i> the Minoans lose their mastery
 over the Mediterranean.</i>

00:14:37.475 --> 00:14:40.613 line:0%
<i> Invaders challenge their power.</i>

00:14:44.082 --> 00:14:48.520 line:0%
<i> And as the sun sets on the
 collapsing Minoan civilization,</i>

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:51.623 line:0%
<i> new powers arise.</i>

00:14:51.623 --> 00:14:55.928 line:0%
<i> 500 miles from Crete,
 just off the coast of Cyprus,</i>

00:14:55.928 --> 00:14:58.330 line:0%
<i> the draining waters
 of the Mediterranean</i>

00:14:58.330 --> 00:15:04.837 line:0%
<i> reveal a remarkable discovery
 almost 2,500 years old.</i>

00:15:06.871 --> 00:15:10.609 line:0%
<i> What can it tell us about
 the lives, the power,</i>

00:15:10.609 --> 00:15:14.479 line:0%
<i> and the pleasures
 of the Ancient Greeks?</i>

00:15:14.479 --> 00:15:17.582 line:0%
<i> In the centuries after
 the fall of the Minoans,</i>

00:15:17.582 --> 00:15:19.284 line:0%
<i> the city-states of Greece</i>

00:15:19.284 --> 00:15:23.722 line:0%
<i> produce dazzling art
 and architecture.</i>

00:15:23.722 --> 00:15:28.393 line:0%
<i> Forging ideas in mathematics,
 democracy and theater</i>

00:15:28.393 --> 00:15:32.631 line:0%
<i> that still shape our world.</i>

00:15:32.631 --> 00:15:36.034 line:0%
<i> The Greeks take to the sea
 in their wooden sailing ships</i>

00:15:36.034 --> 00:15:39.471 line:0%
<i> risking their lives
 to explore, colonize,</i>

00:15:39.471 --> 00:15:41.440 line:0%
<i> and trade with each other.</i>

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:48.313 line:0%
<i> Hundreds of boats shuttle
 across the Mediterranean,</i>

00:15:48.313 --> 00:15:52.984 line:0%
<i> linking settlements in
 Africa, Asia and Europe.</i>

00:15:52.984 --> 00:15:56.655 line:0%
<i> They are the lifeblood
 of Greek civilization.</i>

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:01.560 line:0%
<i> But these ships are a mystery.</i>

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:05.129 line:0%
<i> Only a few remnants have
 survived to offer a glimpse</i>

00:16:05.129 --> 00:16:09.701 line:0%
<i> into how they worked
 and what they carried...</i>

00:16:09.701 --> 00:16:12.837 line:0%
<i> until now.</i>

00:16:12.837 --> 00:16:14.740 line:0%
<i> A shipwreck,
 recently discovered</i>

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:16.775 line:0%
<i> in the seas off Cyprus,</i>

00:16:16.775 --> 00:16:20.713 line:0%
<i> is helping to bring this
 lost world back to life.</i>

00:16:23.715 --> 00:16:27.152 line:0%
<i> Archaeologist Stella Demesticha
 and her team</i>

00:16:27.152 --> 00:16:30.189 line:0%
<i> are unlocking
 the wreck's secrets.</i>

00:16:32.024 --> 00:16:34.993 line:0%
<i> Where did it come from?</i>

00:16:34.993 --> 00:16:37.396 line:0%
<i> What was it carrying?</i>

00:16:40.332 --> 00:16:43.568 line:0%
<i> And why did it sink?</i>

00:16:43.568 --> 00:16:46.505 line:0%
STELLA DEMESTICHA: It's pretty
deep, so it takes a while

00:16:46.505 --> 00:16:50.242 line:0%
when you're diving before
you can see the sea bottom.

00:16:52.611 --> 00:16:54.780 line:0%
SCOTT: It looks all very
higgledy-piggledy,

00:16:54.780 --> 00:17:00.018 line:0%
it looks like, well, someone's
dropped a whole load of garbage

00:17:00.018 --> 00:17:03.221 line:0%
in the ocean.

00:17:03.221 --> 00:17:06.925 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But this apparent
 chaos is packed with clues</i>

00:17:06.925 --> 00:17:10.562 line:0%
<i> about the lost world
 of the Ancient Greeks.</i>

00:17:10.562 --> 00:17:14.699 line:0%
DEMESTICHA: This is really
fantastic for an archaeologist.

00:17:14.699 --> 00:17:17.569 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Exploring such
 a deep and complex site</i>

00:17:17.569 --> 00:17:19.505 line:0%
<i> is challenging.</i>

00:17:21.406 --> 00:17:26.011 line:0%
DEMESTICHA: Diving at 45 meters
has several constraints,

00:17:26.011 --> 00:17:29.715 line:0%
and time is one of them.

00:17:29.715 --> 00:17:34.285 line:0%
The maximum we can stay per day
is 20 minutes.

00:17:34.285 --> 00:17:36.688 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: It's almost
 impossible to work effectively</i>

00:17:36.688 --> 00:17:40.158 line:0%
<i> at such depths.</i>

00:17:40.158 --> 00:17:42.460 line:0%
<i> So the team explores the site</i>

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:45.697 line:0%
<i> using a technique
 called photogrammetry,</i>

00:17:45.697 --> 00:17:47.899 line:0%
<i> taking hundreds of pictures
 of the wreck</i>

00:17:47.899 --> 00:17:50.135 line:0%
<i> from different angles.</i>

00:17:52.638 --> 00:17:58.043 line:0%
DEMESTICHA: So instead of trying
to make decisions at 45 meters

00:17:58.043 --> 00:18:01.313 line:0%
where your brain
doesn't work properly,

00:18:01.313 --> 00:18:05.583 line:0%
taking the pictures allows us
to have the luxury

00:18:05.583 --> 00:18:08.753 line:0%
of diving through the screen
of our computer

00:18:08.753 --> 00:18:11.056 line:0%
as long as we wanted.

00:18:14.025 --> 00:18:17.162 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Using the unique
 photogrammetry data,</i>

00:18:17.162 --> 00:18:19.731 line:0%
<i> it's possible
 for the first time</i>

00:18:19.731 --> 00:18:23.001 line:0%
<i> to drain the Mediterranean...</i>

00:18:23.001 --> 00:18:27.405 line:0%
<i> allowing sunshine to illuminate
 a site that's been in darkness</i>

00:18:27.405 --> 00:18:30.542 line:0%
<i> for 2,500 years.</i>

00:18:30.542 --> 00:18:34.679 line:0%
(music)

00:18:34.679 --> 00:18:38.517 line:0%
<i> The debris lies
 in the shape of a ship.</i>

00:18:41.219 --> 00:18:44.088 line:0%
<i> Much of the timber hull
 has rotted away,</i>

00:18:44.088 --> 00:18:47.092 line:0%
<i> leaving only its ancient cargo.</i>

00:18:49.627 --> 00:18:54.232 line:0%
<i> Hundreds of earthenware jars,
 known as amphorae,</i>

00:18:54.232 --> 00:18:57.068 line:0%
<i> piled neatly on top
 of each other,</i>

00:18:57.068 --> 00:18:59.904 line:0%
<i> many of them still intact.</i>

00:18:59.904 --> 00:19:04.409 line:0%
(music)

00:19:04.409 --> 00:19:05.943 line:0%
SCOTT: Amphorae look very odd.

00:19:05.943 --> 00:19:09.180 line:0%
They look very ungainly
and not very well designed

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:13.618 line:0%
to be storage jars or certainly
container jars on a sea vessel.

00:19:13.618 --> 00:19:16.255 line:0%
But they are a design that's
evolved over centuries.

00:19:16.255 --> 00:19:18.857 line:0%
And actually if you stack them
all really neatly together,

00:19:18.857 --> 00:19:21.059 line:0%
they do all make sense,

00:19:21.059 --> 00:19:24.062 line:0%
and they were the way
that you transported things

00:19:24.062 --> 00:19:27.699 line:0%
around the ancient world.

00:19:27.699 --> 00:19:29.167 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Amphorae like this</i>

00:19:29.167 --> 00:19:34.339 line:0%
<i> give the archaeologists
 some vital clues.</i>

00:19:34.339 --> 00:19:36.742 line:0%
<i> Their distinctive shape varies,</i>

00:19:36.742 --> 00:19:41.346 line:0%
<i> depending on where and when
 they were made.</i>

00:19:41.346 --> 00:19:44.949 line:0%
<i> This one dates from
 the 4th century BC</i>

00:19:44.949 --> 00:19:47.953 line:0%
<i> and comes from
 the Greek island of Chios,</i>

00:19:47.953 --> 00:19:51.556 line:0%
<i> 500 miles from the wreck site.</i>

00:19:51.556 --> 00:19:55.093 line:0%
<i> So what was in it?</i>

00:19:55.093 --> 00:19:58.230 line:0%
<i> Although the amphorae
 are all now empty,</i>

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:02.967 line:0%
<i> they offer intriguing clues
 about what they once contained.</i>

00:20:02.967 --> 00:20:07.572 line:0%
DEMESTICHA: In this case we have
the opportunity to see evidence.

00:20:07.572 --> 00:20:10.775 line:0%
This dark coating
inside the amphora,

00:20:10.775 --> 00:20:14.947 line:0%
we have to imagine that it was
all over the inside walls,

00:20:14.947 --> 00:20:17.315 line:0%
and it was pitch, or resin,

00:20:17.315 --> 00:20:22.553 line:0%
so it was a kind of sealant
to make these walls waterproof.

00:20:22.553 --> 00:20:27.292 line:0%
So we are sure that these
are Chian wine containers.

00:20:27.292 --> 00:20:30.795 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Wine from the island
 of Chios is highly prized</i>

00:20:30.795 --> 00:20:33.532 line:0%
<i> throughout
 the Ancient Mediterranean.</i>

00:20:33.532 --> 00:20:36.134 line:0%
<i> The wrecked ship is loaded
 with the equivalent</i>

00:20:36.134 --> 00:20:40.238 line:0%
<i> of more than 10,000
 modern-sized bottles.</i>

00:20:40.238 --> 00:20:44.208 line:0%
<i> A hugely valuable cargo
 and a telling insight</i>

00:20:44.208 --> 00:20:49.414 line:0%
<i> into the Ancient Greek trade
 in luxury goods.</i>

00:20:49.414 --> 00:20:53.018 line:0%
SCOTT: The Greeks
loved their wine.

00:20:53.018 --> 00:20:54.452 line:0%
This little wreck off Cyprus

00:20:54.452 --> 00:20:57.622 line:0%
is the tip of the iceberg
of the wine trade.

00:20:57.622 --> 00:21:02.560 line:0%
It was an absolutely fundamental
part of their society

00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:04.762 line:0%
and of all their
cultural experiences,

00:21:04.762 --> 00:21:06.765 line:0%
whether that be religious

00:21:06.765 --> 00:21:08.533 line:0%
or whether it be
letting their hair down

00:21:08.533 --> 00:21:11.302 line:0%
and having a really good time.

00:21:11.302 --> 00:21:14.005 line:0%
DEMESTICHA: The greatest thing
about Ancient Greeks

00:21:14.005 --> 00:21:15.940 line:0%
is their love for life.

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:21.279 line:0%
They like to talk, to think,
to discuss, to drink, to party.

00:21:21.279 --> 00:21:23.448 line:0%
SCOTT: There was a great profit
to be made

00:21:23.448 --> 00:21:27.852 line:0%
in making sure that the rich
around the Mediterranean world

00:21:27.852 --> 00:21:31.490 line:0%
had a good supply of
very good wine to drink.

00:21:33.357 --> 00:21:35.960 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Trading in wine
 and other luxury items</i>

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:39.064 line:0%
<i> makes good money for
 the Greek city-states.</i>

00:21:39.064 --> 00:21:42.734 line:0%
<i> But their ships carry
 an even more precious cargo,</i>

00:21:42.734 --> 00:21:46.371 line:0%
<i> as they traverse
 the Mediterranean and beyond,</i>

00:21:46.371 --> 00:21:49.407 line:0%
<i> from Egypt to Southern France,</i>

00:21:49.407 --> 00:21:52.243 line:0%
<i> they spread Greek
 ideas and culture</i>

00:21:52.243 --> 00:21:54.679 line:0%
<i> that influence
 Western civilization</i>

00:21:54.679 --> 00:21:57.682 line:0%
<i> to the present day.</i>

00:21:57.682 --> 00:22:02.820 line:0%
<i> But this cargo never
 reaches its destination.</i>

00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:05.690 line:0%
DEMESTICHA: One of the most
important questions that we ask

00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:09.961 line:0%
in shipwreck archaeology
is why this ship sunk.

00:22:13.131 --> 00:22:17.268 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The biggest clue is
 the shape of the debris.</i>

00:22:17.268 --> 00:22:20.071 line:0%
<i> The way that the jars
 lie grouped together</i>

00:22:20.071 --> 00:22:22.006 line:0%
<i> rather than scattered about</i>

00:22:22.006 --> 00:22:24.843 line:0%
<i> proves that the vessel
 didn't capsize.</i>

00:22:28.313 --> 00:22:31.449 line:0%
<i> So what did happen?</i>

00:22:31.449 --> 00:22:35.687 line:0%
DEMESTICHA: The ships in
antiquity were open-decked.

00:22:35.687 --> 00:22:38.155 line:0%
The hold was not covered
with a deck,

00:22:38.155 --> 00:22:42.393 line:0%
so when the waves were
very high, or we have a storm,

00:22:42.393 --> 00:22:44.896 line:0%
then the water was coming in.

00:22:47.566 --> 00:22:51.169 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The ship is most
 likely overwhelmed by a wave.</i>

00:22:55.073 --> 00:22:58.809 line:0%
<i> Pulled down by the weight
 of all the expensive wine</i>

00:22:58.809 --> 00:23:00.912 line:0%
<i> to a watery grave.</i>

00:23:00.912 --> 00:23:07.752 line:0%
(music)

00:23:07.752 --> 00:23:10.188 line:0%
(music)

00:23:10.188 --> 00:23:15.126 line:0%
(music)

00:23:15.126 --> 00:23:17.228 line:0%
<i> As the waters
 of the Mediterranean</i>

00:23:17.228 --> 00:23:21.032 line:0%
<i> continue to drain away,</i>

00:23:21.032 --> 00:23:25.069 line:0%
<i> they uncover unique evidence
 of a titanic clash</i>

00:23:25.069 --> 00:23:29.473 line:0%
<i> between two ancient
 superpowers.</i>

00:23:29.473 --> 00:23:31.509 line:0%
<i> This is the site of a battle</i>

00:23:31.509 --> 00:23:35.012 line:0%
<i> that would change
 the course of history.</i>

00:23:35.012 --> 00:23:36.914 line:0%
WILLIAM M. MURRAY: When they saw
the Romans in front of them,

00:23:36.914 --> 00:23:40.285 line:0%
they had one of these
'Oh, no!' moments.

00:23:42.687 --> 00:23:46.190 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: What can these
 bizarre objects on the seabed</i>

00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:50.595 line:0%
<i> tell us about the merciless
 rise of the Romans?</i>

00:23:55.233 --> 00:23:58.036 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The third century BC.</i>

00:23:58.036 --> 00:24:01.172 line:0%
<i> Rome already controls
 mainland Italy,</i>

00:24:01.172 --> 00:24:05.877 line:0%
<i> and has ambitions to expand
 across the whole Mediterranean.</i>

00:24:05.877 --> 00:24:08.479 line:0%
<i> But its navy is weak.</i>

00:24:08.479 --> 00:24:10.848 line:0%
JON HENDERSON: Rome was known
as being a terrestrial power,

00:24:10.848 --> 00:24:14.719 line:0%
not a maritime power, it was not
known for fighting sea battles,

00:24:14.719 --> 00:24:16.454 line:0%
but they were set on
a militaristic path

00:24:16.454 --> 00:24:18.657 line:0%
of maritime control.

00:24:18.657 --> 00:24:20.324 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: This brings them
 into conflict</i>

00:24:20.324 --> 00:24:23.694 line:0%
<i> with another superpower
 of the Mediterranean:</i>

00:24:23.694 --> 00:24:27.332 line:0%
<i> the Carthaginians.</i>

00:24:27.332 --> 00:24:30.135 line:0%
SCOTT: The Carthaginians were
great traders, great seafarers,

00:24:30.135 --> 00:24:33.838 line:0%
controlling most of the west
and central Mediterranean,

00:24:33.838 --> 00:24:35.173 line:0%
and it was that fact

00:24:35.173 --> 00:24:39.377 line:0%
that brought them
into conflict with Rome.

00:24:39.377 --> 00:24:41.079 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Carthage,
 from its position</i>

00:24:41.079 --> 00:24:43.247 line:0%
<i> on the north coast of Africa,</i>

00:24:43.247 --> 00:24:47.284 line:0%
<i> commands the most powerful navy
 in the region.</i>

00:24:47.284 --> 00:24:49.020 line:0%
<i> To challenge Carthage,</i>

00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:53.391 line:0%
<i> Rome must first learn
 a new kind of naval warfare.</i>

00:24:56.060 --> 00:24:59.063 line:0%
<i> So begins a titanic struggle</i>

00:24:59.063 --> 00:25:03.201 line:0%
<i> that will last for
 more than 100 years.</i>

00:25:03.201 --> 00:25:06.471 line:0%
<i> The winner will dominate
 the Mediterranean</i>

00:25:06.471 --> 00:25:09.541 line:0%
<i> for the next seven centuries.</i>

00:25:11.242 --> 00:25:14.345 line:0%
<i> Its first truly decisive
 encounter happens</i>

00:25:14.345 --> 00:25:18.883 line:0%
<i> somewhere off
 the west coast of Sicily,</i>

00:25:18.883 --> 00:25:21.920 line:0%
<i> near the Egadi Islands.</i>

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:25.790 line:0%
<i> Here, according to
 ancient historian Polybius,</i>

00:25:25.790 --> 00:25:28.459 line:0%
<i> the mighty navies
 of Rome and Carthage</i>

00:25:28.459 --> 00:25:33.464 line:0%
<i> go head-to-head
 in an epic battle.</i>

00:25:33.464 --> 00:25:36.634 line:0%
<i> He describes the clash
 of two huge fleets</i>

00:25:36.634 --> 00:25:43.475 line:0%
<i> involving 400 ships,
 and at least 100,000 men...</i>

00:25:43.475 --> 00:25:47.846 line:0%
<i> turning the waters of
 the Mediterranean blood-red.</i>

00:25:51.749 --> 00:25:53.751 line:0%
<i> But Polybius writes his history</i>

00:25:53.751 --> 00:25:57.422 line:0%
<i> at least half a century
 after the battle</i>

00:25:57.422 --> 00:26:00.091 line:0%
<i> and for a Roman audience.</i>

00:26:00.091 --> 00:26:04.129 line:0%
<i> So how reliable is his account?</i>

00:26:04.129 --> 00:26:06.898 line:0%
<i> However huge this battle
 may have been,</i>

00:26:06.898 --> 00:26:11.335 line:0%
<i> no one has ever been able
 to find any trace of it.</i>

00:26:11.335 --> 00:26:14.338 line:0%
MURRAY: Taking the description
from an ancient historian

00:26:14.338 --> 00:26:17.708 line:0%
and actually pointing
to the exact place

00:26:17.708 --> 00:26:20.211 line:0%
on the surface of the globe
where the battle took place

00:26:20.211 --> 00:26:23.314 line:0%
is extremely difficult.

00:26:23.314 --> 00:26:26.584 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Bill Murray and
 a team of marine archaeologists</i>

00:26:26.584 --> 00:26:29.687 line:0%
<i> are on the trail of
 some extraordinary finds</i>

00:26:29.687 --> 00:26:33.758 line:0%
<i> reported by local fishermen.</i>

00:26:33.758 --> 00:26:37.127 line:0%
<i> But the area they need
 to survey is huge,</i>

00:26:37.127 --> 00:26:40.798 line:0%
<i> many miles across.</i>

00:26:40.798 --> 00:26:44.569 line:0%
<i> So first, they sonar scan
 the seabed.</i>

00:26:48.372 --> 00:26:51.476 line:0%
<i> Then they launch
 a remotely operated vehicle</i>

00:26:51.476 --> 00:26:53.978 line:0%
<i> to investigate the finds.</i>

00:26:53.978 --> 00:26:56.414 line:0%
(music)

00:26:56.414 --> 00:27:01.386 line:0%
(music)

00:27:01.386 --> 00:27:03.154 line:0%
PETER CAMPBELL: As you're
watching the live feed

00:27:03.154 --> 00:27:06.691 line:0%
of the video from the ROV, and
it's going across the seafloor,

00:27:06.691 --> 00:27:08.393 line:0%
the shapes suddenly
pop into view,

00:27:08.393 --> 00:27:09.927 line:0%
and it's incredibly distinctive.

00:27:09.927 --> 00:27:11.596 line:0%
So there's a great moment
of excitement

00:27:11.596 --> 00:27:14.732 line:0%
as these objects are first seen.

00:27:14.732 --> 00:27:18.035 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: They discover
 a truly astonishing shape</i>

00:27:18.035 --> 00:27:20.338 line:0%
<i> 300 feet down.</i>

00:27:24.075 --> 00:27:25.409 line:0%
CAMPBELL: These are
the rarest artifacts

00:27:25.409 --> 00:27:27.879 line:0%
we have from antiquity.

00:27:30.915 --> 00:27:32.917 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: So what is it?</i>

00:27:36.353 --> 00:27:39.256 line:0%
<i> Using the data gathered
 by the expedition,</i>

00:27:39.256 --> 00:27:42.860 line:0%
<i> it's possible to pull the plug
 on the Mediterranean,</i>

00:27:42.860 --> 00:27:46.397 line:0%
<i> emptying the waters from
 around the coast of Sicily.</i>

00:27:46.397 --> 00:27:51.502 line:0%
<i> The mysterious object on the
 seabed comes clearly into view.</i>

00:27:51.502 --> 00:27:54.939 line:0%
<i> It's three feet wide
 and made of metal.</i>

00:27:54.939 --> 00:27:59.010 line:0%
<i> Its jagged edges suggest
 a deadly purpose.</i>

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:02.647 line:0%
SCOTT: So these are
bronze battering rams.

00:28:02.647 --> 00:28:06.384 line:0%
They would have been attached
to the front of a ship,

00:28:06.384 --> 00:28:08.152 line:0%
and this was the main method

00:28:08.152 --> 00:28:12.390 line:0%
of attacking and destroying
ships in antiquity.

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:14.592 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Rams are
 the superweapons</i>

00:28:14.592 --> 00:28:17.461 line:0%
<i> of ancient naval warfare.</i>

00:28:17.461 --> 00:28:21.399 line:0%
<i> The large vertical fin is
 like a splitting axe,</i>

00:28:21.399 --> 00:28:24.402 line:0%
<i> and the horizontal fins
 are like blades,</i>

00:28:24.402 --> 00:28:27.605 line:0%
<i> to slice through
 an enemy ship's timbers.</i>

00:28:27.605 --> 00:28:29.273 line:0%
SCOTT: You didn't
have cannon fire,

00:28:29.273 --> 00:28:31.909 line:0%
there were no kind of guns
a la Pirates of the Caribbean

00:28:31.909 --> 00:28:33.344 line:0%
or anything like that.

00:28:33.344 --> 00:28:35.179 line:0%
The only way to take down
the enemy ship

00:28:35.179 --> 00:28:37.782 line:0%
was to smash a massive hole
in the middle of it

00:28:37.782 --> 00:28:39.250 line:0%
and let it sink to the bottom,

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:41.519 line:0%
and the only way of doing that
was to ram it.

00:28:41.519 --> 00:28:46.290 line:0%
(crashing)

00:28:46.290 --> 00:28:47.692 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: A closer look reveals</i>

00:28:47.692 --> 00:28:50.327 line:0%
<i> that this ram is
 battle-damaged,</i>

00:28:50.327 --> 00:28:52.830 line:0%
<i> its metal edges
 broken and distorted</i>

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:54.966 line:0%
<i> by a violent collision.</i>

00:28:57.835 --> 00:28:59.237 line:0%
MURRAY: In one episode

00:28:59.237 --> 00:29:02.607 line:0%
we're told that the men
up in a forward tower

00:29:02.607 --> 00:29:05.576 line:0%
were literally catapulted
out of the tower

00:29:05.576 --> 00:29:08.480 line:0%
and into the sea
after a ram strike.

00:29:12.349 --> 00:29:14.519 line:0%
And we're told that it was
an effective ram strike,

00:29:14.519 --> 00:29:18.890 line:0%
because as the ancient author
wrote, 'Bronze hit bronze.'

00:29:25.429 --> 00:29:29.567 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: And this isn't
 the only discovery.</i>

00:29:29.567 --> 00:29:32.069 line:0%
<i> Based on data from the survey,</i>

00:29:32.069 --> 00:29:34.772 line:0%
<i> draining away more
 of the Mediterranean</i>

00:29:34.772 --> 00:29:37.909 line:0%
<i> reveals a remarkable pattern.</i>

00:29:37.909 --> 00:29:40.111 line:0%
<i> Ten more rams, scattered</i>

00:29:40.111 --> 00:29:43.147 line:0%
<i> across two square miles.</i>

00:29:43.147 --> 00:29:46.050 line:0%
<i> More than enough
 to convince historians</i>

00:29:46.050 --> 00:29:49.687 line:0%
<i> that an important naval battle
 took place here.</i>

00:29:51.422 --> 00:29:55.459 line:0%
<i> But is it the legendary
 Battle of the Egadi Islands</i>

00:29:55.459 --> 00:29:57.562 line:0%
<i> described by Polybius?</i>

00:29:59.797 --> 00:30:04.002 line:0%
<i> An unlikely piece of evidence
 may hold the answer.</i>

00:30:06.103 --> 00:30:11.109 line:0%
<i> A single amphora storage jar,
 found near one of the rams.</i>

00:30:13.510 --> 00:30:15.980 line:0%
<i> By analyzing its shape</i>

00:30:15.980 --> 00:30:21.719 line:0%
<i> archaeologists can narrow down
 when and where it was made.</i>

00:30:21.719 --> 00:30:26.023 line:0%
<i> It's like finding
 a black box recorder.</i>

00:30:26.023 --> 00:30:29.260 line:0%
<i> The team believes the amphora
 is Carthaginian</i>

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:32.496 line:0%
<i> and dates
 to the 3rd century BC,</i>

00:30:32.496 --> 00:30:38.069 line:0%
<i> the same period as the Battle
 of the Egadi Islands.</i>

00:30:38.069 --> 00:30:41.672 line:0%
<i> The historian Polybius
 describes how the Carthaginians</i>

00:30:41.672 --> 00:30:46.610 line:0%
<i> are carrying supplies for
 their soldiers in Sicily.</i>

00:30:46.610 --> 00:30:51.382 line:0%
<i> Could this amphora
 be part of that cargo?</i>

00:30:51.382 --> 00:30:53.384 line:0%
POLYBIUS: The plan was to cross
to Mount Erice,

00:30:53.384 --> 00:30:57.288 line:0%
unobserved by the enemy,
and offload the stores.

00:30:57.288 --> 00:30:59.957 line:0%
Then take on soldiers
in the lightened ships

00:30:59.957 --> 00:31:02.093 line:0%
and engage the enemy.

00:31:03.927 --> 00:31:07.331 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But the Carthaginians
 never make it.</i>

00:31:07.331 --> 00:31:10.368 line:0%
<i> Compelling evidence
 from the drained seabed</i>

00:31:10.368 --> 00:31:14.905 line:0%
<i> now indicates this is
 where it all happened.</i>

00:31:14.905 --> 00:31:17.608 line:0%
MURRAY: A number of rams line up
and give us a bearing

00:31:17.608 --> 00:31:20.444 line:0%
that makes some sense,

00:31:20.444 --> 00:31:23.414 line:0%
and that's fascinating,
that is exciting.

00:31:23.414 --> 00:31:25.616 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The words
 of an ancient historian</i>

00:31:25.616 --> 00:31:28.319 line:0%
<i> and the evidence
 of modern technology</i>

00:31:28.319 --> 00:31:31.722 line:0%
<i> both point
 to the same conclusion:</i>

00:31:31.722 --> 00:31:36.227 line:0%
<i> this is the site of the Battle
 of the Egadi Islands.</i>

00:31:38.262 --> 00:31:40.297 line:0%
MURRAY: It was an important
enough event for the Romans

00:31:40.297 --> 00:31:41.799 line:0%
that they remembered the date.

00:31:41.799 --> 00:31:44.435 line:0%
It was actually written down
in a calendar somewhere,

00:31:44.435 --> 00:31:48.806 line:0%
and we know that it occurred
on 10 March, 241 BC.

00:31:51.809 --> 00:31:55.612 line:0%
This is not your typical naval
battle where both sides prepare.

00:31:55.612 --> 00:31:57.982 line:0%
This was a battle
of opportunity.

00:32:00.384 --> 00:32:04.021 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The Carthaginians,
 still laden with supplies,</i>

00:32:04.021 --> 00:32:07.959 line:0%
<i> head for shore believing
 the coast is clear.</i>

00:32:10.194 --> 00:32:13.397 line:0%
<i> But the Romans keep dozens
 of their ships hidden</i>

00:32:13.397 --> 00:32:15.566 line:0%
<i> behind one of the islands.</i>

00:32:15.566 --> 00:32:18.903 line:0%
<i> It's a huge ambush.</i>

00:32:18.903 --> 00:32:22.239 line:0%
MURRAY: The lookouts spied the
Carthaginian ships coming in,

00:32:22.239 --> 00:32:25.042 line:0%
and as the Carthaginians saw
the Romans in front of them,

00:32:25.042 --> 00:32:28.846 line:0%
they had one of these
'Oh, no!' moments.

00:32:28.846 --> 00:32:31.248 line:0%
CAMPBELL: So we're talking about
tens of thousands of men

00:32:31.248 --> 00:32:32.850 line:0%
on board these ships,

00:32:32.850 --> 00:32:34.985 line:0%
and they would have lined up
across from each other

00:32:34.985 --> 00:32:36.621 line:0%
in lines of battle,

00:32:36.621 --> 00:32:38.289 line:0%
headed toward each other
at a great rate,

00:32:38.289 --> 00:32:40.425 line:0%
and smashed into each other
head-to-head.

00:32:42.993 --> 00:32:46.831 line:0%
(crashing)

00:32:46.831 --> 00:32:50.501 line:0%
(men yelling)

00:32:52.670 --> 00:32:57.308 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Polybius describes
 what happened next.</i>

00:32:57.308 --> 00:33:00.310 line:0%
POLYBIUS: Before long,
they were defeated.

00:33:00.310 --> 00:33:03.213 line:0%
50 of the Carthaginians' ships
were sunk,

00:33:03.213 --> 00:33:05.949 line:0%
and 70 captured
with their crews,

00:33:05.949 --> 00:33:10.788 line:0%
while the prisoners
numbered almost 10,000.

00:33:10.788 --> 00:33:14.792 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Another discovery on
 the seabed is a stark reminder</i>

00:33:14.792 --> 00:33:20.064 line:0%
<i> that this epic victory came
 at a high price in human life.</i>

00:33:25.436 --> 00:33:30.308 line:0%
<i> This is the last resting place
 of thousands of brave men.</i>

00:33:33.544 --> 00:33:36.147 line:0%
<i> And it has been judged
 so important</i>

00:33:36.147 --> 00:33:41.185 line:0%
<i> that its exact location
 must remain secret.</i>

00:33:41.185 --> 00:33:42.586 line:0%
CAMPBELL: There's nothing else
really like this.

00:33:42.586 --> 00:33:44.055 line:0%
It's the first
ancient battle site

00:33:44.055 --> 00:33:45.689 line:0%
that's ever been discovered.

00:33:45.689 --> 00:33:49.193 line:0%
It's just an incredible
debris field.

00:33:49.193 --> 00:33:51.429 line:0%
SCOTT: The Battle of the Egadi
Islands was the turning point,

00:33:51.429 --> 00:33:57.368 line:0%
when a Roman fleet managed
to absolutely trounce

00:33:57.368 --> 00:34:00.538 line:0%
a Carthaginian fleet at sea.

00:34:00.538 --> 00:34:02.106 line:0%
CAMPBELL: This was
the defining battle

00:34:02.106 --> 00:34:07.010 line:0%
where Rome went from a regional
power to a superpower.

00:34:07.010 --> 00:34:09.513 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The struggle between
 Rome and Carthage</i>

00:34:09.513 --> 00:34:12.617 line:0%
<i> would last for another century.</i>

00:34:12.617 --> 00:34:15.953 line:0%
<i> But victory here
 set Rome on a path</i>

00:34:15.953 --> 00:34:22.793 line:0%
<i> to shaping the destiny of
 Europe for the next 700 years.</i>

00:34:22.793 --> 00:34:25.696 line:0%
<i> As the last of its water
 runs away,</i>

00:34:25.696 --> 00:34:29.700 line:0%
<i> the Mediterranean reveals
 a final secret.</i>

00:34:29.700 --> 00:34:33.404 line:0%
<i> What can draining
 the Bay of Naples teach us</i>

00:34:33.404 --> 00:34:38.609 line:0%
<i> about the glory and the
 decadence of the Roman Empire?</i>

00:34:43.614 --> 00:34:45.616 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: 2,000 years ago</i>

00:34:45.616 --> 00:34:49.653 line:0%
<i> Rome has grown far beyond
 its Italian homeland.</i>

00:34:49.653 --> 00:34:52.656 line:0%
<i> Its legions control a vast area</i>

00:34:52.656 --> 00:34:56.159 line:0%
<i> from North Africa
 to Northern Europe,</i>

00:34:56.159 --> 00:34:59.330 line:0%
<i> from Spain to the Black Sea,</i>

00:34:59.330 --> 00:35:03.200 line:0%
<i> and the Roman navy dominates
 the Mediterranean,</i>

00:35:03.200 --> 00:35:07.071 line:0%
<i> from its port next
 to the city of Baiae</i>

00:35:07.071 --> 00:35:10.174 line:0%
<i> in the shadow
 of mighty Vesuvius.</i>

00:35:12.676 --> 00:35:17.914 line:0%
<i> Baiae earns a reputation as
 the Sin City of Ancient Rome.</i>

00:35:17.914 --> 00:35:21.886 line:0%
<i> It's famous for debauchery
 and excess.</i>

00:35:24.521 --> 00:35:29.660 line:0%
<i> But what really went on
 in this seaside party town?</i>

00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:34.599 line:0%
<i> And why does so much of it lie
 abandoned beneath the waves?</i>

00:35:37.401 --> 00:35:41.905 line:0%
<i> Now, marine archaeologist
 Jon Henderson is exploring</i>

00:35:41.905 --> 00:35:44.809 line:0%
<i> the secrets
 of this sunken city.</i>

00:35:47.144 --> 00:35:49.847 line:0%
<i> Starting with
 the huge harbor walls</i>

00:35:49.847 --> 00:35:52.884 line:0%
<i> that once surrounded
 the city's port.</i>

00:35:54.718 --> 00:35:57.288 line:0%
HENDERSON: These are built
by the Romans.

00:35:57.288 --> 00:35:59.756 line:0%
They were incredible engineers.

00:35:59.756 --> 00:36:01.958 line:0%
You can still see
the artificial construction

00:36:01.958 --> 00:36:04.094 line:0%
of this very clearly.

00:36:04.094 --> 00:36:08.098 line:0%
You can see the brickwork,
overlapping bricks.

00:36:08.098 --> 00:36:09.867 line:0%
Amazing!

00:36:14.038 --> 00:36:17.808 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Beyond the port walls
 lie magnificent villas</i>

00:36:17.808 --> 00:36:21.812 line:0%
<i> where Roman emperors
 host lavish parties.</i>

00:36:21.812 --> 00:36:23.614 line:0%
HENDERSON: Look at this.

00:36:23.614 --> 00:36:28.552 line:0%
This is a mosaic floor
of one of the bath houses.

00:36:28.552 --> 00:36:30.688 line:0%
Looks like it was
just done yesterday.

00:36:30.688 --> 00:36:32.990 line:0%
It's phenomenal!

00:36:32.990 --> 00:36:35.626 line:0%
Absolutely amazing!

00:36:38.696 --> 00:36:40.931 line:0%
This is called the nymphaeum.

00:36:40.931 --> 00:36:42.966 line:0%
It's actually
an elaborate dining room

00:36:42.966 --> 00:36:44.768 line:0%
for the Emperor Claudius.

00:36:44.768 --> 00:36:46.670 line:0%
You can imagine people
eating food

00:36:46.670 --> 00:36:49.173 line:0%
surrounded by
these water nymphs.

00:36:49.173 --> 00:36:53.311 line:0%
It was built to entertain the
emperor's most honored guests.

00:36:56.513 --> 00:36:57.748 line:0%
Well, one of the things
I'm interested in

00:36:57.748 --> 00:36:59.450 line:0%
is actually the scale
of the site.

00:36:59.450 --> 00:37:03.120 line:0%
Much of the focus has been on
the resort area of Baiae itself.

00:37:03.120 --> 00:37:04.488 line:0%
But on the margins of the site

00:37:04.488 --> 00:37:07.090 line:0%
there are constantly
new areas coming up.

00:37:07.090 --> 00:37:11.962 line:0%
(music)

00:37:11.962 --> 00:37:13.664 line:0%
That's it, slow it down.

00:37:17.334 --> 00:37:19.337 line:0%
There's a lot down there.

00:37:21.305 --> 00:37:24.775 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: So why did the Romans
 lavish so much wealth</i>

00:37:24.775 --> 00:37:29.279 line:0%
<i> on building
 a pleasure city here?</i>

00:37:29.279 --> 00:37:31.015 line:0%
HENDERSON:
The Romans came to Baiae

00:37:31.015 --> 00:37:35.052 line:0%
because of the lovely maritime
climate and the thermal springs.

00:37:35.052 --> 00:37:37.287 line:0%
But Baiae was also
a very important port,

00:37:37.287 --> 00:37:38.422 line:0%
it's a natural port,

00:37:38.422 --> 00:37:40.024 line:0%
it's one of the best ports

00:37:40.024 --> 00:37:43.660 line:0%
on the western coast of Italy
at the time.

00:37:43.660 --> 00:37:46.163 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Wealth brought
 pleasure and excess</i>

00:37:46.163 --> 00:37:50.033 line:0%
<i> to this Las Vegas
 of the Roman world.</i>

00:37:50.033 --> 00:37:52.202 line:0%
HENDERSON: There were parties,
there was drinking,

00:37:52.202 --> 00:37:54.538 line:0%
it was a place
to get prostitutes,

00:37:54.538 --> 00:37:57.941 line:0%
and writers at the time referred
to it as 'a harbor of vice'

00:37:57.941 --> 00:38:01.211 line:0%
or 'a vortex of luxury.'

00:38:01.211 --> 00:38:04.181 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The famous
 Roman philosopher Seneca</i>

00:38:04.181 --> 00:38:07.918 line:0%
<i> is appalled by what he finds.</i>

00:38:07.918 --> 00:38:11.521 line:0%
SENECA: Baiae is a place
to be avoided.

00:38:11.521 --> 00:38:14.791 line:0%
People wandering drunk
along the beach,

00:38:14.791 --> 00:38:18.128 line:0%
the riotous reveling
of sailing parties,

00:38:18.128 --> 00:38:22.032 line:0%
the lakes noisy with singing.

00:38:22.032 --> 00:38:26.003 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: But time is running
 out for this party town.</i>

00:38:28.205 --> 00:38:31.641 line:0%
<i> Gradually many of its most
 impressive public buildings</i>

00:38:31.641 --> 00:38:35.446 line:0%
<i> and private villas are
 lost beneath the waves.</i>

00:38:38.682 --> 00:38:42.052 line:0%
<i> What happened?</i>

00:38:42.052 --> 00:38:44.789 line:0%
<i> Based on detailed
 sonar scanning,</i>

00:38:44.789 --> 00:38:49.626 line:0%
<i> draining away the Mediterranean
 begins to reveal the real story</i>

00:38:49.626 --> 00:38:54.030 line:0%
<i> of the rise and fall of Baiae.</i>

00:38:54.030 --> 00:38:58.668 line:0%
<i> It exposes just how much of
 the city fell under the waves.</i>

00:38:58.668 --> 00:39:02.873 line:0%
<i> 430 acres of streets, shops,</i>

00:39:02.873 --> 00:39:06.277 line:0%
<i> warehouses, and luxury villas.</i>

00:39:08.712 --> 00:39:13.116 line:0%
<i> Draining beyond the town
 uncovers the reason--</i>

00:39:13.116 --> 00:39:15.352 line:0%
<i> an extraordinary landscape,</i>

00:39:15.352 --> 00:39:19.656 line:0%
<i> shaped by powerful
 underground forces.</i>

00:39:19.656 --> 00:39:25.295 line:0%
<i> The whole town sits inside
 the shallow crater</i>

00:39:25.295 --> 00:39:28.465 line:0%
<i> of a giant volcano.</i>

00:39:28.465 --> 00:39:30.133 line:0%
HENDERSON:
You hear about Naples,

00:39:30.133 --> 00:39:32.369 line:0%
people talk about Vesuvius,
they don't seem to realize that

00:39:32.369 --> 00:39:37.508 line:0%
the whole western Bay of Naples
itself is a massive volcano.

00:39:37.508 --> 00:39:41.712 line:0%
We're actually standing
in a volcano now.

00:39:41.712 --> 00:39:44.781 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The people of Baiae
 may not understand</i>

00:39:44.781 --> 00:39:49.686 line:0%
<i> the unpredictable forces
 stirring beneath them.</i>

00:39:49.686 --> 00:39:52.823 line:0%
DOUGAL JERRAM: And we know
we're sat on a volcano.

00:39:52.823 --> 00:39:54.658 line:0%
But it's not
your normal volcano.

00:39:54.658 --> 00:40:00.063 line:0%
It's this much larger and much
more complex volcanic system.

00:40:00.063 --> 00:40:02.966 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: There's remarkable
 evidence of its restless power</i>

00:40:02.966 --> 00:40:08.005 line:0%
<i> three miles away, across the
 bay in the Temple of Serapis.</i>

00:40:11.075 --> 00:40:13.210 line:0%
JERRAM: It's really when you
get into the Roman ruins

00:40:13.210 --> 00:40:15.011 line:0%
that you can see
what's going on.

00:40:15.011 --> 00:40:16.246 line:0%
You have to look closely,
though.

00:40:16.246 --> 00:40:18.315 line:0%
If you look up
at the column behind me,

00:40:18.315 --> 00:40:19.650 line:0%
you'll see in the middle part

00:40:19.650 --> 00:40:22.119 line:0%
it's got this strange
texture to it.

00:40:22.119 --> 00:40:24.954 line:0%
Lots and lots of holes in it.

00:40:24.954 --> 00:40:26.690 line:0%
There's even bits of shell
on this.

00:40:26.690 --> 00:40:29.326 line:0%
This is a stone-boring
marine mollusk.

00:40:29.326 --> 00:40:33.830 line:0%
That tells me that this stuff
has been under the sea.

00:40:33.830 --> 00:40:35.231 line:0%
Time enough for those mollusks

00:40:35.231 --> 00:40:36.967 line:0%
to get to grips
with the columns.

00:40:36.967 --> 00:40:39.503 line:0%
But look now,
these columns are in place,

00:40:39.503 --> 00:40:42.038 line:0%
and look where those
marker horizons are now.

00:40:42.038 --> 00:40:44.208 line:0%
That's like a tide
on the side of a bath

00:40:44.208 --> 00:40:46.409 line:0%
telling you where
the sea level was.

00:40:46.409 --> 00:40:50.247 line:0%
So we know that this has been
dropped under the sea,

00:40:50.247 --> 00:40:52.516 line:0%
and it's now risen above.

00:40:52.516 --> 00:40:54.751 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Can draining
 the waters of the bay</i>

00:40:54.751 --> 00:40:58.355 line:0%
<i> even further explain why
 this ancient landscape</i>

00:40:58.355 --> 00:41:02.826 line:0%
<i> is constantly rising
 and falling,</i>

00:41:02.826 --> 00:41:08.265 line:0%
<i> and finally show why the Romans
 lost their Sin City?</i>

00:41:15.739 --> 00:41:20.244 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Baiae,
 once a major Roman port,</i>

00:41:20.244 --> 00:41:25.448 line:0%
<i> lies half-submerged amid
 a volcanic landscape.</i>

00:41:25.448 --> 00:41:29.453 line:0%
(music)

00:41:29.453 --> 00:41:31.921 line:0%
<i> Jon Henderson has been
 exploring the area</i>

00:41:31.921 --> 00:41:34.758 line:0%
<i> beneath the waves,</i>

00:41:34.758 --> 00:41:39.696 line:0%
<i> and he finds a telltale sign
 of the power at work here.</i>

00:41:39.696 --> 00:41:40.864 line:0%
HENDERSON: So we've got
all these bubbles

00:41:40.864 --> 00:41:43.600 line:0%
are coming out of the ground,

00:41:43.600 --> 00:41:47.671 line:0%
all this hot air coming up
from the seabed.

00:41:50.740 --> 00:41:54.677 line:0%
You really get a sense here
of the power that's underneath,

00:41:54.677 --> 00:41:57.848 line:0%
waiting to burst out.

00:41:57.848 --> 00:42:01.619 line:0%
Hot water piling out
of the ground under the sea.

00:42:03.353 --> 00:42:06.323 line:0%
I've never seen
anything like this.

00:42:06.323 --> 00:42:09.026 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: These underwater
 vents are superheated</i>

00:42:09.026 --> 00:42:12.228 line:0%
<i> by molten rock
 from the volcano below,</i>

00:42:12.228 --> 00:42:15.798 line:0%
<i> evidence that the forces
 stirring under Baiae</i>

00:42:15.798 --> 00:42:18.469 line:0%
<i> are highly active.</i>

00:42:18.469 --> 00:42:21.672 line:0%
<i> Locals call the area
 Campi Flegri,</i>

00:42:21.672 --> 00:42:24.708 line:0%
<i> the Fields of Fire.</i>

00:42:24.708 --> 00:42:28.111 line:0%
<i> Now, for the first time,
 draining the water</i>

00:42:28.111 --> 00:42:34.184 line:0%
<i> from the entire bay exposes
 an extraordinary site.</i>

00:42:34.184 --> 00:42:39.823 line:0%
<i> 24 separate volcanoes
 nesting inside the crater.</i>

00:42:41.792 --> 00:42:46.163 line:0%
<i> It spans an incredible
 38 square miles.</i>

00:42:48.264 --> 00:42:50.467 line:0%
<i> But geologists here
 have been mapping</i>

00:42:50.467 --> 00:42:54.104 line:0%
<i> deep inside the Earth itself.</i>

00:42:54.104 --> 00:42:57.474 line:0%
<i> Now, draining not only
 the Mediterranean,</i>

00:42:57.474 --> 00:43:01.611 line:0%
<i> but looking deep into
 the layers of rock below it,</i>

00:43:01.611 --> 00:43:05.248 line:0%
<i> reveals a fantastical sight.</i>

00:43:05.248 --> 00:43:08.618 line:0%
<i> Giant cauldrons of
 superheated molten rock,</i>

00:43:08.618 --> 00:43:11.054 line:0%
<i> known as a magma chambers,</i>

00:43:11.054 --> 00:43:15.492 line:0%
<i> that slowly empty and fill
 over centuries.</i>

00:43:15.492 --> 00:43:19.029 line:0%
<i> Evidence that this
 huge volcanic system</i>

00:43:19.029 --> 00:43:22.733 line:0%
<i> is still very much alive.</i>

00:43:22.733 --> 00:43:24.434 line:0%
HENDERSON: Basically you've got
magma chambers

00:43:24.434 --> 00:43:27.638 line:0%
sitting under the sea, which
operate almost like bellows.

00:43:27.638 --> 00:43:30.540 line:0%
When they fill full of lava,
the ground goes up,

00:43:30.540 --> 00:43:32.676 line:0%
and then when they empty again
the ground goes down,

00:43:32.676 --> 00:43:35.412 line:0%
and this process is
going on constantly.

00:43:35.412 --> 00:43:38.815 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: It's as if the land
 within the Campi Flegri crater</i>

00:43:38.815 --> 00:43:41.986 line:0%
<i> itself is breathing.</i>

00:43:43.453 --> 00:43:44.855 line:0%
SCOTT: You feel like
you're standing

00:43:44.855 --> 00:43:49.393 line:0%
on some kind of giant's chest.

00:43:49.393 --> 00:43:54.230 line:0%
You feel minute compared
to the giant tectonic forces

00:43:54.230 --> 00:43:56.332 line:0%
that are creating
the world around you.

00:43:56.332 --> 00:43:57.768 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The damage caused</i>

00:43:57.768 --> 00:44:00.637 line:0%
<i> by these breathing
 chambers of magma</i>

00:44:00.637 --> 00:44:05.142 line:0%
<i> coincides with the decline
 of the Roman Empire.</i>

00:44:05.142 --> 00:44:08.878 line:0%
<i> As part of the city slides
 beneath the Mediterranean,</i>

00:44:08.878 --> 00:44:13.617 line:0%
<i> the population shrinks
 and the parties end;</i>

00:44:13.617 --> 00:44:17.588 line:0%
<i> Sin City is no more.</i>

00:44:19.889 --> 00:44:23.160 line:0%
<i> Today, the scientists studying
 the landscape here</i>

00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:25.929 line:0%
<i> believe that pressures
 in the magma chambers</i>

00:44:25.929 --> 00:44:28.198 line:0%
<i> are increasing once again,</i>

00:44:28.198 --> 00:44:31.301 line:0%
<i> and that could have
 catastrophic consequences</i>

00:44:31.301 --> 00:44:35.171 line:0%
<i> for the millions of people
 living close by.</i>

00:44:35.171 --> 00:44:36.940 line:0%
JERRAM: We know
it's an active system.

00:44:36.940 --> 00:44:38.875 line:0%
So one of the interesting
problems we have

00:44:38.875 --> 00:44:42.245 line:0%
with something the size
of Campi Flegri is,

00:44:42.245 --> 00:44:46.850 line:0%
is it going to erupt big or
is it going to erupt small?

00:44:46.850 --> 00:44:51.055 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR:
 Big could mean very big.</i>

00:44:53.290 --> 00:44:58.495 line:0%
<i> In AD 79,
 nearby Vesuvius erupts.</i>

00:44:58.495 --> 00:45:02.833 line:0%
<i> It destroys
 the city of Pompeii.</i>

00:45:02.833 --> 00:45:07.971 line:0%
<i> Thousands of people burn to
 death or choke on volcanic ash.</i>

00:45:10.207 --> 00:45:12.609 line:0%
<i> But scientists know
 the volcanic system</i>

00:45:12.609 --> 00:45:16.346 line:0%
<i> feeding the Fields of Fire
 is far larger</i>

00:45:16.346 --> 00:45:20.650 line:0%
<i> and far more powerful
 than Vesuvius.</i>

00:45:20.650 --> 00:45:23.386 line:0%
<i> And with so many people
 living nearby,</i>

00:45:23.386 --> 00:45:25.822 line:0%
<i> it's vital to keep a close eye</i>

00:45:25.822 --> 00:45:30.661 line:0%
<i> on the breathing giant
 beneath them.</i>

00:45:30.661 --> 00:45:31.862 line:0%
JERRAM: It's even more critical

00:45:31.862 --> 00:45:33.964 line:0%
that we monitor
volcanoes like this,

00:45:33.964 --> 00:45:35.865 line:0%
and that's because we're verging

00:45:35.865 --> 00:45:38.902 line:0%
on the side of what we call
a supervolcano.

00:45:38.902 --> 00:45:41.871 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: Even the smallest
 changes in the ground level,</i>

00:45:41.871 --> 00:45:45.509 line:0%
<i> or its temperature, could be
 a vital warning sign</i>

00:45:45.509 --> 00:45:51.081 line:0%
<i> that the unstoppable forces of
 nature are about to turn ugly.</i>

00:45:52.782 --> 00:45:54.117 line:0%
JERRAM: If we had an eruption

00:45:54.117 --> 00:45:56.920 line:0%
where these multiple craters
evacuated,

00:45:56.920 --> 00:45:59.289 line:0%
it would be devastating.

00:45:59.289 --> 00:46:03.526 line:0%
It would essentially
flatten this area.

00:46:03.526 --> 00:46:07.597 line:0%
<i> NARRATOR: The eruption could be
 almost ten times more powerful</i>

00:46:07.597 --> 00:46:11.868 line:0%
<i> than the one at Santorini that
 shattered the Minoan world.</i>

00:46:11.868 --> 00:46:17.540 line:0%
(music)

00:46:17.540 --> 00:46:19.776 line:0%
<i> Vast amounts
 of volcanic material</i>

00:46:19.776 --> 00:46:22.579 line:0%
<i> would be ejected
 into the atmosphere,</i>

00:46:22.579 --> 00:46:27.351 line:0%
<i> blocking out the sunlight.</i>

00:46:27.351 --> 00:46:29.753 line:0%
<i> Global temperatures
 would plummet</i>

00:46:29.753 --> 00:46:32.489 line:0%
<i> with devastating consequences.</i>

00:46:36.526 --> 00:46:40.097 line:0%
<i> New scanning technology
 reveals remarkable evidence</i>

00:46:40.097 --> 00:46:44.334 line:0%
<i> lying hidden beneath the waters
 of the Mediterranean.</i>

00:46:44.334 --> 00:46:46.302 line:0%
<i> The raw forces of nature</i>

00:46:46.302 --> 00:46:49.540 line:0%
<i> still shaping
 this volcanic landscape.</i>

00:46:51.508 --> 00:46:57.347 line:0%
<i> Priceless evidence of the trade
 that drove the ancient world,</i>

00:46:57.347 --> 00:47:03.220 line:0%
<i> and an epic battle that changed
 the course of history.</i>

